---
title: React Chat UI
id: typed-headless-ui-react
order: 1
description: "Build a typed, headless React chat UI with createChatHook. Your chat options control the types of tools, parts, and interrupts."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - createChatHook
  - react
  - headless ui
  - useAppChat
  - ToolProps
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-react"},{"type":"text","value":". Import the UI factory from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-react/ui"},{"type":"text","value":". Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChatHook({ options, ...components })"},{"type":"text","value":" once at module scope. This matches Form "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormHook"},{"type":"text","value":" and Table "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":": widgets register on the factory, mix onto Part / Interrupt / Input, and automatic dispatch still walks the message list."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deprecated."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Do not install "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-react-ui"},{"type":"text","value":". That package re-exports this subpath until 1.0.0. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../migration/create-ui","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chat UI packages"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The factory returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAppChat"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChatContext"},{"type":"text","value":". Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAppChat()"},{"type":"text","value":" in the screen to create the instance. Render "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<chat.AppChat />"},{"type":"text","value":". Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChatContext()"},{"type":"text","value":" inside a mapped component when it needs live chat. That value is the instance that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat.AppChat"},{"type":"text","value":" provides."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You supply every visible component. There is no default markup, style, or copy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The factory needs a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolsComponents"},{"type":"text","value":" entry for every tool name in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatOptions"},{"type":"text","value":". It also needs an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interruptsComponents.generic"},{"type":"text","value":" entry for every interrupt id. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generic.fallback"},{"type":"text","value":" is optional."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want a smaller starting point? The "},{"type":"link","href":"./recipes/index","children":[{"type":"text","value":"chat UI recipes"}]},{"type":"text","value":" build one thing at a time, from a plain chat box up to per-request context."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const json: unknown = await request.json()\n  if (typeof json !== 'object' || json === null || !('messages' in json)) {\n    return new Response('Invalid body', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  const messages = json.messages\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.6'),\n    messages: Array.isArray(messages) ? messages : [],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { defineInterrupt, toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Look up weather',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst purchaseItem = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchaseItem',\n  description: 'Buy an item',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ item: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ ok: z.boolean() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst choosePlan = defineInterrupt({\n  id: 'choosePlan',\n  payloadSchema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n  responseSchema: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [getWeather, purchaseItem],\n  interrupts: [choosePlan],\n  outputSchema: z.object({ answer: z.string() }),\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat, useChatContext } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n  layout: function Layout({ Messages, Interrupts, Input, Queue }) {\n    const chat = useChatContext()\n    if (chat.error) return <p>{chat.error.message}</p>\n    if (chat.isLoading && chat.messages.length === 0) return <p>Loading</p>\n    if (chat.messages.length === 0) return <p>Empty</p>\n    return (\n      <main>\n        <Messages />\n        <Interrupts />\n        <Queue />\n        <Input />\n      </main>\n    )\n  },\n  message: function Message({ message, Parts }) {\n    return <article data-role={message.role}><Parts /></article>\n  },\n  input: function Input() {\n    const chat = useChatContext()\n    return (\n      <form\n        onSubmit={(event) => {\n          event.preventDefault()\n          const form = event.currentTarget\n          const field = form.elements.namedItem('message')\n          if (!(field instanceof HTMLInputElement)) return\n          void chat.sendMessage(field.value)\n          field.value = ''\n        }}\n      >\n        <input name=\"message\" />\n        <button type=\"submit\">Send</button>\n      </form>\n    )\n  },\n  queue: function QueueItem({ item }) {\n    const label = typeof item.content === 'string' ? item.content : 'Queued'\n    return (\n      <div>\n        {label}\n        <button type=\"button\" onClick={() => item.cancelQueued()}>\n          Cancel\n        </button>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n  },\n  partsComponents: {\n  text: ({ part }) => <p>{part.content}</p>,\n  structuredOutput: ({ part }) => <pre>{part.raw}</pre>,\n  toolResult: ({ part }) => <em>{String(part.content)}</em>,\n  fallback: ({ part }) => <span>{part.type}</span>,\n  },\n  toolsComponents: {\n  getWeather: ({ part, result }) => {\n    if (part.state === 'awaiting-input') return <p>Waiting</p>\n    if (part.state === 'input-streaming') return <p>Streaming input</p>\n    if (part.state === 'input-complete') return <p>{part.input?.city}</p>\n    if (part.state === 'approval-requested') return <p>Need approval</p>\n    if (part.state === 'approval-responded') return <p>Responded</p>\n    if (part.state === 'error') return <p>Error</p>\n    return (\n      <p>\n        {part.input?.city}: {String(part.output?.temperature ?? result?.content)}\n      </p>\n    )\n  },\n  purchaseItem: ({ part, interrupt }) => (\n    <div>\n      {part.input?.item}\n      {interrupt?.status === 'pending' ? (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}>\n          Approve\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  ),\n  },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n  generic: {\n    choosePlan: ({ interrupt }) => (\n      <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt('approved')}>\n        {interrupt.payload?.title ?? 'Choose plan'}\n      </button>\n    ),\n    fallback: ({ interrupt }) => <p>{interrupt.reason}</p>,\n  },\n  },\n})\n\nexport function Support() {\n  const chat = useAppChat({ threadId: 'support-1' })\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n\nexport function Sidebar() {\n  const chat = useAppChat({ threadId: 'sidebar' })\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Queue />"},{"type":"text","value":" maps pending sends. Register "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queue"},{"type":"text","value":" on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"components"},{"type":"text","value":". Each item has "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancelQueued()"},{"type":"text","value":" bound, so you do not pass the id. Type a queue item with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueueProps<typeof chatOptions>"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout"},{"type":"text","value":" receives "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Messages"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Queue"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Input"},{"type":"text","value":" as components. Render them as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Messages />"},{"type":"text","value":", not as calls. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Input"},{"type":"text","value":" is only on the props when the config registers an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input"},{"type":"text","value":", so rendering an input you never registered is a compile error instead of a silent no-op."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"List "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input"},{"type":"text","value":" before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":" When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input"},{"type":"text","value":" is an inline "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"function"},{"type":"text","value":" expression written "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"after"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout"},{"type":"text","value":", TypeScript cannot yet tell that an input is registered, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Input"},{"type":"text","value":" goes missing from the layout props. Putting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input"},{"type":"text","value":" first fixes it; so does using an arrow or a named reference. If it does slip through, rendering "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Input />"},{"type":"text","value":" without a registered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input"},{"type":"text","value":" warns once in development rather than failing silently."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-a-component-in-its-own-file","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type a component in its own file"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A tool map grows fast. Move a tool into its own file and type the props with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolProps"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolProps"},{"type":"text","value":" takes your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" type and the tool name. Then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.input"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.output"},{"type":"text","value":" stay exact."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Part components work the same way. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PartProps<typeof chatOptions, 'text'>"},{"type":"text","value":" already has a text part. You do not check "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.type"},{"type":"text","value":". Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'structuredOutput'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'thinking'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'toolResult'"},{"type":"text","value":", and the other keys from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partsComponents"},{"type":"text","value":" map. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fallback"},{"type":"text","value":" still sees every part type."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook, type PartProps, type ToolProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Look up weather',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [getWeather],\n}\n\nexport function WeatherTool({\n  part,\n  result,\n}: ToolProps<typeof chatOptions, 'getWeather'>) {\n  if (part.state === 'awaiting-input') return <p>Waiting</p>\n  if (part.state === 'input-streaming') return <p>Streaming input</p>\n  if (part.state === 'error') return <p>Error</p>\n  return (\n    <p>\n      {part.input?.city}: {String(part.output?.temperature ?? result?.content)}\n    </p>\n  )\n}\n\nexport function TextPart({ part }: PartProps<typeof chatOptions, 'text'>) {\n  return <p>{part.content}</p>\n}\n\nexport const { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages }) => <Messages />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { text: TextPart, fallback: () => null },\n  toolsComponents: { getWeather: WeatherTool },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" in a shared module."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolProps"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PartProps"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-react/ui"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type the component with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolProps<typeof chatOptions, 'getWeather'>"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PartProps<typeof chatOptions, 'text'>"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass that component into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools.getWeather"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parts.text"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For an interrupt, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InterruptProps"},{"type":"text","value":". Pass a tool name or a registered interrupt id as the second type argument. Then you do not check "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt.kind"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A tool approval: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'purchaseItem'>"},{"type":"text","value":". Then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt.toolName"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'purchaseItem'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A registered generic interrupt: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'choosePlan'>"},{"type":"text","value":". Then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt.payload"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt.resolveInterrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" match the definition."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook, type InterruptProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { defineInterrupt } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst choosePlan = defineInterrupt({\n  id: 'choosePlan',\n  payloadSchema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n  responseSchema: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  interrupts: [choosePlan],\n}\n\nexport function ChoosePlan({\n  interrupt,\n}: InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'choosePlan'>) {\n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt('approved')}>\n      {interrupt.payload?.title ?? 'Choose plan'}\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n\nexport const { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Interrupts }) => <Interrupts />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n    generic: {\n      choosePlan: ChoosePlan,\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other prop types from the same package:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LayoutProps"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MessageProps"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InputProps"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueueProps"},{"type":"text","value":" for one queued send. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"item.cancelQueued()"},{"type":"text","value":" drops that item."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PartProps"},{"type":"text","value":" with a part key such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'text'"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InterruptProps"},{"type":"text","value":" for tool approvals, registered generic interrupts, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generic.fallback"},{"type":"text","value":". Pass a tool name or interrupt id as the second type argument."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"read-chat-from-usechatcontext","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read chat from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChatContext()"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mapped components do not receive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat"},{"type":"text","value":" as a prop. Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChatContext()"},{"type":"text","value":" inside a component when it needs live chat. That call opts the component into chat re-renders. Nested children can call it too. Widgets in other files should call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChatHookContexts()"},{"type":"text","value":" first so they do not import the factory result (circular import)."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n}\n\nfunction StatusLine() {\n  const chat = useChatContext()\n  if (chat.error) return <p>{chat.error.message}</p>\n  if (chat.isLoading) return <p>Loading</p>\n  return <p>{chat.messages.length} messages</p>\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat, useChatContext } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages }) => (\n      <main>\n        <StatusLine />\n        <Messages />\n      </main>\n    ),\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n})\n\nexport function ChatScreen() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChatContext()"},{"type":"text","value":" only inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AppChat"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Provider"},{"type":"text","value":". A call outside that tree throws."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAppChat"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChatHook"},{"type":"text","value":" owns the state. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChatContext()"},{"type":"text","value":" only reads the instance that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat.AppChat"},{"type":"text","value":" provides."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a widget lives in another file, call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChatHookContexts()"},{"type":"text","value":" first and pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatContext"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partContext"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interruptContext"},{"type":"text","value":" under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":". Then that file can import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChatContext"},{"type":"text","value":" from the contexts module."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Part and interrupt widgets take "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" as props. Type them with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PartProps"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolProps"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InterruptProps"},{"type":"text","value":". Do not read those values from context."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-approvals-inline-or-list","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool approvals: inline or list"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A tool with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":" can render its approval in two places."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"inline-next-to-the-tool","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inline, next to the tool"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" on the tool. Render the approval in that same component. Do not register "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts.tools"},{"type":"text","value":" for that name. A mapped tool keeps its approval off the list."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" is already the approval for that tool name. You do not check "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt.kind"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook, type ToolProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst purchaseItem = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchaseItem',\n  description: 'Buy an item',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ item: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ ok: z.boolean() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [purchaseItem],\n}\n\nfunction PurchaseItem({\n  part,\n  interrupt,\n}: ToolProps<typeof chatOptions, 'purchaseItem'>) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {part.input?.item}\n      {interrupt?.status === 'pending' ? (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}>\n          Approve\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages, Interrupts }) => (\n      <main>\n        <Messages />\n        <Interrupts />\n      </main>\n    ),\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  toolsComponents: {\n    purchaseItem: PurchaseItem,\n  },\n})\n\nexport function InlineApprovalChat() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To split the approval into its own file, type it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'purchaseItem'>"},{"type":"text","value":". Render that component from the tool."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"list-in-interrupts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"List, in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Interrupts />"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Register the approval under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts.tools"},{"type":"text","value":". That component appears in the interrupt list. Do not also render "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" on the tool unless you want it in both places."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst purchaseItem = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchaseItem',\n  description: 'Buy an item',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ item: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ ok: z.boolean() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [purchaseItem],\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages, Interrupts }) => (\n      <main>\n        <Messages />\n        <Interrupts />\n      </main>\n    ),\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  toolsComponents: {\n    purchaseItem: ({ part }) => <div>{part.input?.item}</div>,\n  },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n    tools: {\n      purchaseItem: ({ interrupt }) => (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}>\n          Approve\n        </button>\n      ),\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport function ListApprovalChat() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"generic-interrupts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generic interrupts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generic interrupts always render in the list ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<Interrupts />"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<UI.Interrupts>"},{"type":"text","value":"). They never render inside a tool."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Map them under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interruptsComponents.generic"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A registered id such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"choosePlan"},{"type":"text","value":": the component for that definition"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fallback"},{"type":"text","value":": every other list interrupt, including an unknown generic id and an unbound interrupt this chat does not own"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { defineInterrupt } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst choosePlan = defineInterrupt({\n  id: 'choosePlan',\n  payloadSchema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n  responseSchema: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  interrupts: [choosePlan],\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Interrupts }) => <Interrupts />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n    generic: {\n      choosePlan: ({ interrupt }) => (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt('approved')}>\n          {interrupt.payload?.title ?? 'Choose plan'}\n        </button>\n      ),\n      fallback: ({ interrupt }) =>\n        interrupt.kind === 'unbound' ? (\n          <p>Paused elsewhere: {interrupt.reason}</p>\n        ) : (\n          <p>{interrupt.reason}</p>\n        ),\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport function GenericInterruptChat() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can mix this map with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts.tools"},{"type":"text","value":" in the same factory call."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"manual-traversal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual traversal"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChatUI"},{"type":"text","value":" is the lower-level kit. Use it when you walk messages yourself. Screens still call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChatHook"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatUI } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Look up weather',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [getWeather],\n}\n\nconst UI = createChatUI(chatOptions, {\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages }) => <Messages />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: {\n    text: ({ part }) => <p>{part.content}</p>,\n    fallback: () => null,\n  },\n  toolsComponents: {\n    getWeather: ({ part }) => <p>{part.input?.city}</p>,\n  },\n})\n\nexport function ManualChat() {\n  const chat = useChat(chatOptions)\n  return (\n    <UI.Provider chat={chat}>\n      <UI.Messages>\n        {(messages) =>\n          messages.map((message) => (\n            <UI.Message key={message.id} message={message}>\n              {(parts) =>\n                parts.map((part, index) => (\n                  <span key={index}>{part.key}</span>\n                ))\n              }\n            </UI.Message>\n          ))\n        }\n      </UI.Messages>\n    </UI.Provider>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx","meta":"group=manual-traversal"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unknown runtime tool names warn once in development and render nothing. Add a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parts.fallback"},{"type":"text","value":" for unknown part types."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic dispatch is the default. You can also pick a registered widget at the call site, like Form "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field.TextField"},{"type":"text","value":" and Table "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.TextCell"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { MessageProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nfunction PickedWidgets({\n  message,\n}: {\n  message: MessageProps<typeof chatOptions>['message']\n}) {\n  return (\n    <UI.Message message={message}>\n      {(parts) =>\n        parts.map((part, index) => (\n          <UI.Part key={index} part={part}>\n            {(p) => (part.key === 'toolCall' ? <p.getWeather /> : <p.Render />)}\n          </UI.Part>\n        ))\n      }\n    </UI.Message>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx","meta":"group=manual-traversal"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"p.getWeather"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"p.text"},{"type":"text","value":" are the widgets you passed to the factory. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"p.Render"},{"type":"text","value":" walks this one part the automatic way. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UI.Input"},{"type":"text","value":" is mixed onto the kit when you register "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If widgets live in other files, call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChatHookContexts()"},{"type":"text","value":" first and pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatContext"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partContext"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interruptContext"},{"type":"text","value":" under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":". That breaks the circular import, the same way Form uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormHookContexts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See also "},{"type":"link","href":"./solid","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./vue","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vue"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./svelte","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"link","href":"./custom-adapters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"custom adapters"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"render-markdown","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render markdown"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model replies arrive as markdown. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TextPart"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-react/ui"},{"type":"text","value":" renders it with "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/markdown","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Markdown"}]},{"type":"text","value":". By default, raw HTML is escaped and executable URLs are removed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { TextPart } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nexport function Reply({ content }: { content: string }) {\n  return <TextPart content={content} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fenced code stays plain until you pass a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"highlighter"},{"type":"text","value":". Build one with TanStack Highlight: "},{"type":"link","href":"./markdown","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Highlight markdown code"}]},{"type":"text","value":". A custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"highlighter"},{"type":"text","value":" returns trusted HTML. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTanStackMarkdownHighlighter"},{"type":"text","value":", or another highlighter that escapes source text."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"highlighter"},{"type":"text","value":": a synchronous "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CodeHighlighter"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extensions"},{"type":"text","value":": extra TanStack Markdown extensions. The streaming extension is always on."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"components"},{"type":"text","value":": a tag-name map with normal HTML props ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"href"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"},{"type":"text","value":"). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inline"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"node"},{"type":"text","value":" are not passed."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { TextPart } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nexport function Reply({ content }: { content: string }) {\n  return (\n    <TextPart\n      content={content}\n      components={{\n        a: (props) => <a {...props} target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" />,\n      }}\n    />\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you still pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remarkPlugins"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rehypePlugins"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"disableDefaultPlugins"},{"type":"text","value":", see "},{"type":"link","href":"../migration/text-part-markdown","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TextPart markdown"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"server","text":"Server","level":2},{"id":"client","text":"Client","level":2},{"id":"type-a-component-in-its-own-file","text":"Type a component in its own file","level":2},{"id":"read-chat-from-usechatcontext","text":"Read chat from useChatContext()","level":2},{"id":"tool-approvals-inline-or-list","text":"Tool approvals: inline or list","level":2},{"id":"inline-next-to-the-tool","text":"Inline, next to the tool","level":3},{"id":"list-in-interrupts","text":"List, in <Interrupts />","level":3},{"id":"generic-interrupts","text":"Generic interrupts","level":2},{"id":"manual-traversal","text":"Manual traversal","level":2},{"id":"render-markdown","text":"Render markdown","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nInstall `@tanstack/ai-react`. Import the UI factory from `@tanstack/ai-react/ui`. Call `createChatHook({ options, ...components })` once at module scope. This matches Form `createFormHook` and Table `createTableHook`: widgets register on the factory, mix onto Part / Interrupt / Input, and automatic dispatch still walks the message list.\n\n> **Deprecated.** Do not install `@tanstack/ai-react-ui`. That package re-exports this subpath until 1.0.0. See [Chat UI packages](../migration/create-ui).\n\nThe factory returns `useAppChat` and `useChatContext`. Call `useAppChat()` in the screen to create the instance. Render `<chat.AppChat />`. Call `useChatContext()` inside a mapped component when it needs live chat. That value is the instance that `chat.AppChat` provides.\n\nYou supply every visible component. There is no default markup, style, or copy.\n\nThe factory needs a `toolsComponents` entry for every tool name in `chatOptions`. It also needs an `interruptsComponents.generic` entry for every interrupt id. `generic.fallback` is optional.\n\nWant a smaller starting point? The [chat UI recipes](./recipes/index) build one thing at a time, from a plain chat box up to per-request context.\n\n## Server\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const json: unknown = await request.json()\n  if (typeof json !== 'object' || json === null || !('messages' in json)) {\n    return new Response('Invalid body', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  const messages = json.messages\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.6'),\n    messages: Array.isArray(messages) ? messages : [],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n## Client\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { defineInterrupt, toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Look up weather',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst purchaseItem = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchaseItem',\n  description: 'Buy an item',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ item: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ ok: z.boolean() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst choosePlan = defineInterrupt({\n  id: 'choosePlan',\n  payloadSchema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n  responseSchema: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [getWeather, purchaseItem],\n  interrupts: [choosePlan],\n  outputSchema: z.object({ answer: z.string() }),\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat, useChatContext } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n  layout: function Layout({ Messages, Interrupts, Input, Queue }) {\n    const chat = useChatContext()\n    if (chat.error) return <p>{chat.error.message}</p>\n    if (chat.isLoading && chat.messages.length === 0) return <p>Loading</p>\n    if (chat.messages.length === 0) return <p>Empty</p>\n    return (\n      <main>\n        <Messages />\n        <Interrupts />\n        <Queue />\n        <Input />\n      </main>\n    )\n  },\n  message: function Message({ message, Parts }) {\n    return <article data-role={message.role}><Parts /></article>\n  },\n  input: function Input() {\n    const chat = useChatContext()\n    return (\n      <form\n        onSubmit={(event) => {\n          event.preventDefault()\n          const form = event.currentTarget\n          const field = form.elements.namedItem('message')\n          if (!(field instanceof HTMLInputElement)) return\n          void chat.sendMessage(field.value)\n          field.value = ''\n        }}\n      >\n        <input name=\"message\" />\n        <button type=\"submit\">Send</button>\n      </form>\n    )\n  },\n  queue: function QueueItem({ item }) {\n    const label = typeof item.content === 'string' ? item.content : 'Queued'\n    return (\n      <div>\n        {label}\n        <button type=\"button\" onClick={() => item.cancelQueued()}>\n          Cancel\n        </button>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n  },\n  partsComponents: {\n  text: ({ part }) => <p>{part.content}</p>,\n  structuredOutput: ({ part }) => <pre>{part.raw}</pre>,\n  toolResult: ({ part }) => <em>{String(part.content)}</em>,\n  fallback: ({ part }) => <span>{part.type}</span>,\n  },\n  toolsComponents: {\n  getWeather: ({ part, result }) => {\n    if (part.state === 'awaiting-input') return <p>Waiting</p>\n    if (part.state === 'input-streaming') return <p>Streaming input</p>\n    if (part.state === 'input-complete') return <p>{part.input?.city}</p>\n    if (part.state === 'approval-requested') return <p>Need approval</p>\n    if (part.state === 'approval-responded') return <p>Responded</p>\n    if (part.state === 'error') return <p>Error</p>\n    return (\n      <p>\n        {part.input?.city}: {String(part.output?.temperature ?? result?.content)}\n      </p>\n    )\n  },\n  purchaseItem: ({ part, interrupt }) => (\n    <div>\n      {part.input?.item}\n      {interrupt?.status === 'pending' ? (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}>\n          Approve\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  ),\n  },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n  generic: {\n    choosePlan: ({ interrupt }) => (\n      <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt('approved')}>\n        {interrupt.payload?.title ?? 'Choose plan'}\n      </button>\n    ),\n    fallback: ({ interrupt }) => <p>{interrupt.reason}</p>,\n  },\n  },\n})\n\nexport function Support() {\n  const chat = useAppChat({ threadId: 'support-1' })\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n\nexport function Sidebar() {\n  const chat = useAppChat({ threadId: 'sidebar' })\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\n`<Queue />` maps pending sends. Register `queue` on `components`. Each item has `cancelQueued()` bound, so you do not pass the id. Type a queue item with `QueueProps<typeof chatOptions>`.\n\n`layout` receives `Messages`, `Interrupts`, `Queue`, and `Input` as components. Render them as `<Messages />`, not as calls. `Input` is only on the props when the config registers an `input`, so rendering an input you never registered is a compile error instead of a silent no-op.\n\n> **List `input` before `layout`.** When `input` is an inline `function` expression written *after* `layout`, TypeScript cannot yet tell that an input is registered, and `Input` goes missing from the layout props. Putting `input` first fixes it; so does using an arrow or a named reference. If it does slip through, rendering `<Input />` without a registered `input` warns once in development rather than failing silently.\n\n## Type a component in its own file\n\nA tool map grows fast. Move a tool into its own file and type the props with `ToolProps`.\n\n`ToolProps` takes your `chatOptions` type and the tool name. Then `part.input` and `part.output` stay exact.\n\nPart components work the same way. `PartProps<typeof chatOptions, 'text'>` already has a text part. You do not check `part.type`. Use `'structuredOutput'`, `'thinking'`, `'toolResult'`, and the other keys from the `partsComponents` map. `fallback` still sees every part type.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook, type PartProps, type ToolProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Look up weather',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [getWeather],\n}\n\nexport function WeatherTool({\n  part,\n  result,\n}: ToolProps<typeof chatOptions, 'getWeather'>) {\n  if (part.state === 'awaiting-input') return <p>Waiting</p>\n  if (part.state === 'input-streaming') return <p>Streaming input</p>\n  if (part.state === 'error') return <p>Error</p>\n  return (\n    <p>\n      {part.input?.city}: {String(part.output?.temperature ?? result?.content)}\n    </p>\n  )\n}\n\nexport function TextPart({ part }: PartProps<typeof chatOptions, 'text'>) {\n  return <p>{part.content}</p>\n}\n\nexport const { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages }) => <Messages />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { text: TextPart, fallback: () => null },\n  toolsComponents: { getWeather: WeatherTool },\n})\n```\n\n1. Put `chatOptions` in a shared module.\n2. Import `ToolProps` or `PartProps` from `@tanstack/ai-react/ui`.\n3. Type the component with `ToolProps<typeof chatOptions, 'getWeather'>` or `PartProps<typeof chatOptions, 'text'>`.\n4. Pass that component into `tools.getWeather` or `parts.text`.\n\nFor an interrupt, use `InterruptProps`. Pass a tool name or a registered interrupt id as the second type argument. Then you do not check `interrupt.kind`.\n\n- A tool approval: `InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'purchaseItem'>`. Then `interrupt.toolName` is `'purchaseItem'`.\n- A registered generic interrupt: `InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'choosePlan'>`. Then `interrupt.payload` and `interrupt.resolveInterrupt` match the definition.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook, type InterruptProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { defineInterrupt } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst choosePlan = defineInterrupt({\n  id: 'choosePlan',\n  payloadSchema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n  responseSchema: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  interrupts: [choosePlan],\n}\n\nexport function ChoosePlan({\n  interrupt,\n}: InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'choosePlan'>) {\n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt('approved')}>\n      {interrupt.payload?.title ?? 'Choose plan'}\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n\nexport const { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Interrupts }) => <Interrupts />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n    generic: {\n      choosePlan: ChoosePlan,\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nOther prop types from the same package:\n\n- `LayoutProps`\n- `MessageProps`\n- `InputProps`\n- `QueueProps` for one queued send. `item.cancelQueued()` drops that item.\n- `PartProps` with a part key such as `'text'`\n- `InterruptProps` for tool approvals, registered generic interrupts, and `generic.fallback`. Pass a tool name or interrupt id as the second type argument.\n\n## Read chat from `useChatContext()`\n\nMapped components do not receive `chat` as a prop. Call `useChatContext()` inside a component when it needs live chat. That call opts the component into chat re-renders. Nested children can call it too. Widgets in other files should call `createChatHookContexts()` first so they do not import the factory result (circular import).\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n}\n\nfunction StatusLine() {\n  const chat = useChatContext()\n  if (chat.error) return <p>{chat.error.message}</p>\n  if (chat.isLoading) return <p>Loading</p>\n  return <p>{chat.messages.length} messages</p>\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat, useChatContext } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages }) => (\n      <main>\n        <StatusLine />\n        <Messages />\n      </main>\n    ),\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n})\n\nexport function ChatScreen() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\nCall `useChatContext()` only inside `AppChat` or `Provider`. A call outside that tree throws.\n\n`useAppChat` from `createChatHook` owns the state. `useChatContext()` only reads the instance that `chat.AppChat` provides.\n\nWhen a widget lives in another file, call `createChatHookContexts()` first and pass `chatContext`, `partContext`, and `interruptContext` under `context`. Then that file can import `useChatContext` from the contexts module.\n\nPart and interrupt widgets take `part` and `interrupt` as props. Type them with `PartProps`, `ToolProps`, or `InterruptProps`. Do not read those values from context.\n\n## Tool approvals: inline or list\n\nA tool with `needsApproval: true` can render its approval in two places.\n\n### Inline, next to the tool\n\nRead `interrupt` on the tool. Render the approval in that same component. Do not register `interrupts.tools` for that name. A mapped tool keeps its approval off the list.\n\n`interrupt` is already the approval for that tool name. You do not check `interrupt.kind`.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook, type ToolProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst purchaseItem = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchaseItem',\n  description: 'Buy an item',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ item: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ ok: z.boolean() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [purchaseItem],\n}\n\nfunction PurchaseItem({\n  part,\n  interrupt,\n}: ToolProps<typeof chatOptions, 'purchaseItem'>) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {part.input?.item}\n      {interrupt?.status === 'pending' ? (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}>\n          Approve\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages, Interrupts }) => (\n      <main>\n        <Messages />\n        <Interrupts />\n      </main>\n    ),\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  toolsComponents: {\n    purchaseItem: PurchaseItem,\n  },\n})\n\nexport function InlineApprovalChat() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\nTo split the approval into its own file, type it with `InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'purchaseItem'>`. Render that component from the tool.\n\n### List, in `<Interrupts />`\n\nRegister the approval under `interrupts.tools`. That component appears in the interrupt list. Do not also render `interrupt` on the tool unless you want it in both places.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst purchaseItem = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchaseItem',\n  description: 'Buy an item',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ item: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ ok: z.boolean() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [purchaseItem],\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages, Interrupts }) => (\n      <main>\n        <Messages />\n        <Interrupts />\n      </main>\n    ),\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  toolsComponents: {\n    purchaseItem: ({ part }) => <div>{part.input?.item}</div>,\n  },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n    tools: {\n      purchaseItem: ({ interrupt }) => (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}>\n          Approve\n        </button>\n      ),\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport function ListApprovalChat() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\n## Generic interrupts\n\nGeneric interrupts always render in the list (`<Interrupts />` / `<UI.Interrupts>`). They never render inside a tool.\n\nMap them under `interruptsComponents.generic`:\n\n- A registered id such as `choosePlan`: the component for that definition\n- `fallback`: every other list interrupt, including an unknown generic id and an unbound interrupt this chat does not own\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { defineInterrupt } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst choosePlan = defineInterrupt({\n  id: 'choosePlan',\n  payloadSchema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n  responseSchema: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  interrupts: [choosePlan],\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Interrupts }) => <Interrupts />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n    generic: {\n      choosePlan: ({ interrupt }) => (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt('approved')}>\n          {interrupt.payload?.title ?? 'Choose plan'}\n        </button>\n      ),\n      fallback: ({ interrupt }) =>\n        interrupt.kind === 'unbound' ? (\n          <p>Paused elsewhere: {interrupt.reason}</p>\n        ) : (\n          <p>{interrupt.reason}</p>\n        ),\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport function GenericInterruptChat() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\nYou can mix this map with `interrupts.tools` in the same factory call.\n\n## Manual traversal\n\n`createChatUI` is the lower-level kit. Use it when you walk messages yourself. Screens still call `createChatHook`.\n\n```tsx group=manual-traversal\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatUI } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Look up weather',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [getWeather],\n}\n\nconst UI = createChatUI(chatOptions, {\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages }) => <Messages />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: {\n    text: ({ part }) => <p>{part.content}</p>,\n    fallback: () => null,\n  },\n  toolsComponents: {\n    getWeather: ({ part }) => <p>{part.input?.city}</p>,\n  },\n})\n\nexport function ManualChat() {\n  const chat = useChat(chatOptions)\n  return (\n    <UI.Provider chat={chat}>\n      <UI.Messages>\n        {(messages) =>\n          messages.map((message) => (\n            <UI.Message key={message.id} message={message}>\n              {(parts) =>\n                parts.map((part, index) => (\n                  <span key={index}>{part.key}</span>\n                ))\n              }\n            </UI.Message>\n          ))\n        }\n      </UI.Messages>\n    </UI.Provider>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nUnknown runtime tool names warn once in development and render nothing. Add a `parts.fallback` for unknown part types.\n\nAutomatic dispatch is the default. You can also pick a registered widget at the call site, like Form `field.TextField` and Table `cell.TextCell`:\n\n```tsx group=manual-traversal\nimport type { MessageProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nfunction PickedWidgets({\n  message,\n}: {\n  message: MessageProps<typeof chatOptions>['message']\n}) {\n  return (\n    <UI.Message message={message}>\n      {(parts) =>\n        parts.map((part, index) => (\n          <UI.Part key={index} part={part}>\n            {(p) => (part.key === 'toolCall' ? <p.getWeather /> : <p.Render />)}\n          </UI.Part>\n        ))\n      }\n    </UI.Message>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n`p.getWeather` and `p.text` are the widgets you passed to the factory. `p.Render` walks this one part the automatic way. `UI.Input` is mixed onto the kit when you register `input`.\n\nIf widgets live in other files, call `createChatHookContexts()` first and pass `chatContext`, `partContext`, and `interruptContext` under `context`. That breaks the circular import, the same way Form uses `createFormHookContexts`.\n\nSee also [Solid](./solid), [Vue](./vue), [Svelte](./svelte), and [custom adapters](./custom-adapters).\n\n## Render markdown\n\nModel replies arrive as markdown. `TextPart` from `@tanstack/ai-react/ui` renders it with [TanStack Markdown](https://tanstack.com/markdown). By default, raw HTML is escaped and executable URLs are removed.\n\n```tsx\nimport { TextPart } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nexport function Reply({ content }: { content: string }) {\n  return <TextPart content={content} />\n}\n```\n\nFenced code stays plain until you pass a `highlighter`. Build one with TanStack Highlight: [Highlight markdown code](./markdown). A custom `highlighter` returns trusted HTML. Use `createTanStackMarkdownHighlighter`, or another highlighter that escapes source text.\n\n- `highlighter`: a synchronous `CodeHighlighter`.\n- `extensions`: extra TanStack Markdown extensions. The streaming extension is always on.\n- `components`: a tag-name map with normal HTML props (`href`, `className`). `inline` and `node` are not passed.\n\n```tsx\nimport { TextPart } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nexport function Reply({ content }: { content: string }) {\n  return (\n    <TextPart\n      content={content}\n      components={{\n        a: (props) => <a {...props} target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" />,\n      }}\n    />\n  )\n}\n```\n\nIf you still pass `remarkPlugins`, `rehypePlugins`, or `disableDefaultPlugins`, see [TextPart markdown](../migration/text-part-markdown).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: React Chat UI\nid: typed-headless-ui-react\norder: 1\ndescription: \"Build a typed, headless React chat UI with createChatHook. Your chat options control the types of tools, parts, and interrupts.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - createChatHook\n  - react\n  - headless ui\n  - useAppChat\n  - ToolProps\n---\n\nInstall `@tanstack/ai-react`. Import the UI factory from `@tanstack/ai-react/ui`. Call `createChatHook({ options, ...components })` once at module scope. This matches Form `createFormHook` and Table `createTableHook`: widgets register on the factory, mix onto Part / Interrupt / Input, and automatic dispatch still walks the message list.\n\n> **Deprecated.** Do not install `@tanstack/ai-react-ui`. That package re-exports this subpath until 1.0.0. See [Chat UI packages](../migration/create-ui).\n\nThe factory returns `useAppChat` and `useChatContext`. Call `useAppChat()` in the screen to create the instance. Render `<chat.AppChat />`. Call `useChatContext()` inside a mapped component when it needs live chat. That value is the instance that `chat.AppChat` provides.\n\nYou supply every visible component. There is no default markup, style, or copy.\n\nThe factory needs a `toolsComponents` entry for every tool name in `chatOptions`. It also needs an `interruptsComponents.generic` entry for every interrupt id. `generic.fallback` is optional.\n\nWant a smaller starting point? The [chat UI recipes](./recipes/index) build one thing at a time, from a plain chat box up to per-request context.\n\n## Server\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const json: unknown = await request.json()\n  if (typeof json !== 'object' || json === null || !('messages' in json)) {\n    return new Response('Invalid body', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  const messages = json.messages\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.6'),\n    messages: Array.isArray(messages) ? messages : [],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n## Client\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { defineInterrupt, toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Look up weather',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst purchaseItem = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchaseItem',\n  description: 'Buy an item',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ item: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ ok: z.boolean() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst choosePlan = defineInterrupt({\n  id: 'choosePlan',\n  payloadSchema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n  responseSchema: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [getWeather, purchaseItem],\n  interrupts: [choosePlan],\n  outputSchema: z.object({ answer: z.string() }),\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat, useChatContext } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n  layout: function Layout({ Messages, Interrupts, Input, Queue }) {\n    const chat = useChatContext()\n    if (chat.error) return <p>{chat.error.message}</p>\n    if (chat.isLoading && chat.messages.length === 0) return <p>Loading</p>\n    if (chat.messages.length === 0) return <p>Empty</p>\n    return (\n      <main>\n        <Messages />\n        <Interrupts />\n        <Queue />\n        <Input />\n      </main>\n    )\n  },\n  message: function Message({ message, Parts }) {\n    return <article data-role={message.role}><Parts /></article>\n  },\n  input: function Input() {\n    const chat = useChatContext()\n    return (\n      <form\n        onSubmit={(event) => {\n          event.preventDefault()\n          const form = event.currentTarget\n          const field = form.elements.namedItem('message')\n          if (!(field instanceof HTMLInputElement)) return\n          void chat.sendMessage(field.value)\n          field.value = ''\n        }}\n      >\n        <input name=\"message\" />\n        <button type=\"submit\">Send</button>\n      </form>\n    )\n  },\n  queue: function QueueItem({ item }) {\n    const label = typeof item.content === 'string' ? item.content : 'Queued'\n    return (\n      <div>\n        {label}\n        <button type=\"button\" onClick={() => item.cancelQueued()}>\n          Cancel\n        </button>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n  },\n  partsComponents: {\n  text: ({ part }) => <p>{part.content}</p>,\n  structuredOutput: ({ part }) => <pre>{part.raw}</pre>,\n  toolResult: ({ part }) => <em>{String(part.content)}</em>,\n  fallback: ({ part }) => <span>{part.type}</span>,\n  },\n  toolsComponents: {\n  getWeather: ({ part, result }) => {\n    if (part.state === 'awaiting-input') return <p>Waiting</p>\n    if (part.state === 'input-streaming') return <p>Streaming input</p>\n    if (part.state === 'input-complete') return <p>{part.input?.city}</p>\n    if (part.state === 'approval-requested') return <p>Need approval</p>\n    if (part.state === 'approval-responded') return <p>Responded</p>\n    if (part.state === 'error') return <p>Error</p>\n    return (\n      <p>\n        {part.input?.city}: {String(part.output?.temperature ?? result?.content)}\n      </p>\n    )\n  },\n  purchaseItem: ({ part, interrupt }) => (\n    <div>\n      {part.input?.item}\n      {interrupt?.status === 'pending' ? (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}>\n          Approve\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  ),\n  },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n  generic: {\n    choosePlan: ({ interrupt }) => (\n      <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt('approved')}>\n        {interrupt.payload?.title ?? 'Choose plan'}\n      </button>\n    ),\n    fallback: ({ interrupt }) => <p>{interrupt.reason}</p>,\n  },\n  },\n})\n\nexport function Support() {\n  const chat = useAppChat({ threadId: 'support-1' })\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n\nexport function Sidebar() {\n  const chat = useAppChat({ threadId: 'sidebar' })\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\n`<Queue />` maps pending sends. Register `queue` on `components`. Each item has `cancelQueued()` bound, so you do not pass the id. Type a queue item with `QueueProps<typeof chatOptions>`.\n\n`layout` receives `Messages`, `Interrupts`, `Queue`, and `Input` as components. Render them as `<Messages />`, not as calls. `Input` is only on the props when the config registers an `input`, so rendering an input you never registered is a compile error instead of a silent no-op.\n\n> **List `input` before `layout`.** When `input` is an inline `function` expression written *after* `layout`, TypeScript cannot yet tell that an input is registered, and `Input` goes missing from the layout props. Putting `input` first fixes it; so does using an arrow or a named reference. If it does slip through, rendering `<Input />` without a registered `input` warns once in development rather than failing silently.\n\n## Type a component in its own file\n\nA tool map grows fast. Move a tool into its own file and type the props with `ToolProps`.\n\n`ToolProps` takes your `chatOptions` type and the tool name. Then `part.input` and `part.output` stay exact.\n\nPart components work the same way. `PartProps<typeof chatOptions, 'text'>` already has a text part. You do not check `part.type`. Use `'structuredOutput'`, `'thinking'`, `'toolResult'`, and the other keys from the `partsComponents` map. `fallback` still sees every part type.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook, type PartProps, type ToolProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Look up weather',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [getWeather],\n}\n\nexport function WeatherTool({\n  part,\n  result,\n}: ToolProps<typeof chatOptions, 'getWeather'>) {\n  if (part.state === 'awaiting-input') return <p>Waiting</p>\n  if (part.state === 'input-streaming') return <p>Streaming input</p>\n  if (part.state === 'error') return <p>Error</p>\n  return (\n    <p>\n      {part.input?.city}: {String(part.output?.temperature ?? result?.content)}\n    </p>\n  )\n}\n\nexport function TextPart({ part }: PartProps<typeof chatOptions, 'text'>) {\n  return <p>{part.content}</p>\n}\n\nexport const { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages }) => <Messages />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { text: TextPart, fallback: () => null },\n  toolsComponents: { getWeather: WeatherTool },\n})\n```\n\n1. Put `chatOptions` in a shared module.\n2. Import `ToolProps` or `PartProps` from `@tanstack/ai-react/ui`.\n3. Type the component with `ToolProps<typeof chatOptions, 'getWeather'>` or `PartProps<typeof chatOptions, 'text'>`.\n4. Pass that component into `tools.getWeather` or `parts.text`.\n\nFor an interrupt, use `InterruptProps`. Pass a tool name or a registered interrupt id as the second type argument. Then you do not check `interrupt.kind`.\n\n- A tool approval: `InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'purchaseItem'>`. Then `interrupt.toolName` is `'purchaseItem'`.\n- A registered generic interrupt: `InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'choosePlan'>`. Then `interrupt.payload` and `interrupt.resolveInterrupt` match the definition.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook, type InterruptProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { defineInterrupt } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst choosePlan = defineInterrupt({\n  id: 'choosePlan',\n  payloadSchema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n  responseSchema: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  interrupts: [choosePlan],\n}\n\nexport function ChoosePlan({\n  interrupt,\n}: InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'choosePlan'>) {\n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt('approved')}>\n      {interrupt.payload?.title ?? 'Choose plan'}\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n\nexport const { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Interrupts }) => <Interrupts />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n    generic: {\n      choosePlan: ChoosePlan,\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nOther prop types from the same package:\n\n- `LayoutProps`\n- `MessageProps`\n- `InputProps`\n- `QueueProps` for one queued send. `item.cancelQueued()` drops that item.\n- `PartProps` with a part key such as `'text'`\n- `InterruptProps` for tool approvals, registered generic interrupts, and `generic.fallback`. Pass a tool name or interrupt id as the second type argument.\n\n## Read chat from `useChatContext()`\n\nMapped components do not receive `chat` as a prop. Call `useChatContext()` inside a component when it needs live chat. That call opts the component into chat re-renders. Nested children can call it too. Widgets in other files should call `createChatHookContexts()` first so they do not import the factory result (circular import).\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n}\n\nfunction StatusLine() {\n  const chat = useChatContext()\n  if (chat.error) return <p>{chat.error.message}</p>\n  if (chat.isLoading) return <p>Loading</p>\n  return <p>{chat.messages.length} messages</p>\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat, useChatContext } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages }) => (\n      <main>\n        <StatusLine />\n        <Messages />\n      </main>\n    ),\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n})\n\nexport function ChatScreen() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\nCall `useChatContext()` only inside `AppChat` or `Provider`. A call outside that tree throws.\n\n`useAppChat` from `createChatHook` owns the state. `useChatContext()` only reads the instance that `chat.AppChat` provides.\n\nWhen a widget lives in another file, call `createChatHookContexts()` first and pass `chatContext`, `partContext`, and `interruptContext` under `context`. Then that file can import `useChatContext` from the contexts module.\n\nPart and interrupt widgets take `part` and `interrupt` as props. Type them with `PartProps`, `ToolProps`, or `InterruptProps`. Do not read those values from context.\n\n## Tool approvals: inline or list\n\nA tool with `needsApproval: true` can render its approval in two places.\n\n### Inline, next to the tool\n\nRead `interrupt` on the tool. Render the approval in that same component. Do not register `interrupts.tools` for that name. A mapped tool keeps its approval off the list.\n\n`interrupt` is already the approval for that tool name. You do not check `interrupt.kind`.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook, type ToolProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst purchaseItem = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchaseItem',\n  description: 'Buy an item',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ item: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ ok: z.boolean() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [purchaseItem],\n}\n\nfunction PurchaseItem({\n  part,\n  interrupt,\n}: ToolProps<typeof chatOptions, 'purchaseItem'>) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {part.input?.item}\n      {interrupt?.status === 'pending' ? (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}>\n          Approve\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages, Interrupts }) => (\n      <main>\n        <Messages />\n        <Interrupts />\n      </main>\n    ),\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  toolsComponents: {\n    purchaseItem: PurchaseItem,\n  },\n})\n\nexport function InlineApprovalChat() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\nTo split the approval into its own file, type it with `InterruptProps<typeof chatOptions, 'purchaseItem'>`. Render that component from the tool.\n\n### List, in `<Interrupts />`\n\nRegister the approval under `interrupts.tools`. That component appears in the interrupt list. Do not also render `interrupt` on the tool unless you want it in both places.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst purchaseItem = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchaseItem',\n  description: 'Buy an item',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ item: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ ok: z.boolean() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [purchaseItem],\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages, Interrupts }) => (\n      <main>\n        <Messages />\n        <Interrupts />\n      </main>\n    ),\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  toolsComponents: {\n    purchaseItem: ({ part }) => <div>{part.input?.item}</div>,\n  },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n    tools: {\n      purchaseItem: ({ interrupt }) => (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}>\n          Approve\n        </button>\n      ),\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport function ListApprovalChat() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\n## Generic interrupts\n\nGeneric interrupts always render in the list (`<Interrupts />` / `<UI.Interrupts>`). They never render inside a tool.\n\nMap them under `interruptsComponents.generic`:\n\n- A registered id such as `choosePlan`: the component for that definition\n- `fallback`: every other list interrupt, including an unknown generic id and an unbound interrupt this chat does not own\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatHook } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { defineInterrupt } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst choosePlan = defineInterrupt({\n  id: 'choosePlan',\n  payloadSchema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n  responseSchema: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  interrupts: [choosePlan],\n}\n\nconst { useAppChat } = createChatHook({\n  options: chatOptions,\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Interrupts }) => <Interrupts />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: { fallback: () => null },\n  interruptsComponents: {\n    generic: {\n      choosePlan: ({ interrupt }) => (\n        <button onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt('approved')}>\n          {interrupt.payload?.title ?? 'Choose plan'}\n        </button>\n      ),\n      fallback: ({ interrupt }) =>\n        interrupt.kind === 'unbound' ? (\n          <p>Paused elsewhere: {interrupt.reason}</p>\n        ) : (\n          <p>{interrupt.reason}</p>\n        ),\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport function GenericInterruptChat() {\n  const chat = useAppChat()\n  return <chat.AppChat />\n}\n```\n\nYou can mix this map with `interrupts.tools` in the same factory call.\n\n## Manual traversal\n\n`createChatUI` is the lower-level kit. Use it when you walk messages yourself. Screens still call `createChatHook`.\n\n```tsx group=manual-traversal\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { createChatUI } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Look up weather',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number() }),\n}).client()\n\nconst chatOptions = {\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  tools: [getWeather],\n}\n\nconst UI = createChatUI(chatOptions, {\n  components: {\n    layout: ({ Messages }) => <Messages />,\n    message: ({ Parts }) => <article><Parts /></article>,\n  },\n  partsComponents: {\n    text: ({ part }) => <p>{part.content}</p>,\n    fallback: () => null,\n  },\n  toolsComponents: {\n    getWeather: ({ part }) => <p>{part.input?.city}</p>,\n  },\n})\n\nexport function ManualChat() {\n  const chat = useChat(chatOptions)\n  return (\n    <UI.Provider chat={chat}>\n      <UI.Messages>\n        {(messages) =>\n          messages.map((message) => (\n            <UI.Message key={message.id} message={message}>\n              {(parts) =>\n                parts.map((part, index) => (\n                  <span key={index}>{part.key}</span>\n                ))\n              }\n            </UI.Message>\n          ))\n        }\n      </UI.Messages>\n    </UI.Provider>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nUnknown runtime tool names warn once in development and render nothing. Add a `parts.fallback` for unknown part types.\n\nAutomatic dispatch is the default. You can also pick a registered widget at the call site, like Form `field.TextField` and Table `cell.TextCell`:\n\n```tsx group=manual-traversal\nimport type { MessageProps } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nfunction PickedWidgets({\n  message,\n}: {\n  message: MessageProps<typeof chatOptions>['message']\n}) {\n  return (\n    <UI.Message message={message}>\n      {(parts) =>\n        parts.map((part, index) => (\n          <UI.Part key={index} part={part}>\n            {(p) => (part.key === 'toolCall' ? <p.getWeather /> : <p.Render />)}\n          </UI.Part>\n        ))\n      }\n    </UI.Message>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n`p.getWeather` and `p.text` are the widgets you passed to the factory. `p.Render` walks this one part the automatic way. `UI.Input` is mixed onto the kit when you register `input`.\n\nIf widgets live in other files, call `createChatHookContexts()` first and pass `chatContext`, `partContext`, and `interruptContext` under `context`. That breaks the circular import, the same way Form uses `createFormHookContexts`.\n\nSee also [Solid](./solid), [Vue](./vue), [Svelte](./svelte), and [custom adapters](./custom-adapters).\n\n## Render markdown\n\nModel replies arrive as markdown. `TextPart` from `@tanstack/ai-react/ui` renders it with [TanStack Markdown](https://tanstack.com/markdown). By default, raw HTML is escaped and executable URLs are removed.\n\n```tsx\nimport { TextPart } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nexport function Reply({ content }: { content: string }) {\n  return <TextPart content={content} />\n}\n```\n\nFenced code stays plain until you pass a `highlighter`. Build one with TanStack Highlight: [Highlight markdown code](./markdown). A custom `highlighter` returns trusted HTML. Use `createTanStackMarkdownHighlighter`, or another highlighter that escapes source text.\n\n- `highlighter`: a synchronous `CodeHighlighter`.\n- `extensions`: extra TanStack Markdown extensions. The streaming extension is always on.\n- `components`: a tag-name map with normal HTML props (`href`, `className`). `inline` and `node` are not passed.\n\n```tsx\nimport { TextPart } from '@tanstack/ai-react/ui'\n\nexport function Reply({ content }: { content: string }) {\n  return (\n    <TextPart\n      content={content}\n      components={{\n        a: (props) => <a {...props} target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" />,\n      }}\n    />\n  )\n}\n```\n\nIf you still pass `remarkPlugins`, `rehypePlugins`, or `disableDefaultPlugins`, see [TextPart markdown](../migration/text-part-markdown).\n"}
